[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is Fleet, the open-source device management. Every laptop, desktop, phone, and server your organization owns enrolled, patched, secured, and queryable from one dashboard. macOS, Windows, Linux, Chromebooks, iOS,'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Android, even your cloud instances and containers. It\'s the platform managing more than 2,000,000 devices across 1,300 organizations. Uber, Stripe, Proton, Grafana Labs, even Mr. Beast\'s crew. And'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s the part no proprietary MDM can say. Your employees can read the agent source code and verify exactly what IT can and cannot collect. There is a catch about which parts are free. So, you end'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll draw that line clearly at the end. Scale first because in this category scale is credibility. More than 2,000,000 computing devices run Fleet today across 1,300 organizations in 90-plus countries. The largest single'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'deployment manages over 400,000 devices. One company, one Fleet instance. In enterprise device management, that is not a promising open-source experiment looking for its first believers. That\'s'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'production infrastructure carrying a decade of accumulated trust. The lineage explains the trust. Fleet descends from osquery, the device visibility engine born at Facebook that turned operating systems into SQL databases. And'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'osquery\'s co-creator, Zach Wasserman, is Fleet\'s CTO. The project began life as Kolide Fleet back in 2017, went quiet, and in 2020 Zach teamed up with Sails.js creator Mike McNeal to revive it as'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Fleet Device Management. Today, it\'s an all-remote company with 467 contributors and a release train deep into version 4.90. What it actually does in one list. It enrolls devices into real MDM,'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'configuration profiles, disk encryption, OS settings over the air. It patches operating systems and deploys software across every platform. It runs scripts remotely, shell on Mac and Linux, PowerShell on Windows. It answers live'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'questions about any machine through osquery. What\'s installed, what\'s running, what\'s plugged in, hundreds of tables of ground truth, not stale inventory spreadsheets. It tracks vulnerabilities across the whole fleet'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and ships CIS benchmarks for audit-ready compliance evidence. And you can drive all of it four ways: a clean UI, a REST API, webhooks, or pure GitOps YAML sitting in version control. For the'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'terminal people, fleetctl is the entire platform in a CLI. Live query every host in the company with SQL. Yes, actual SQL against your laptops. Script it, pipe it, and keep your entire device'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'configuration in YAML that you apply like a Kubernetes manifest. This is IT as software engineering instead of IT as clicking through a vendor console and hoping. If you\'ve ever written a bash loop over'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an SSH host list to answer one auditor question, this section alone justifies the migration. Here\'s the culture shift fleet represents. Traditional MDM is a black box on every laptop. Employees have no idea what it collects. IT clicks'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through a vendor console and the vendor\'s road map is your road map. Take it or leave it. Fleet flips every single one of those. The agent is open source, so anyone, including the employees being managed, can read exactly what it does.'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Configuration lives in version controlled YAML with pull requests and code review. And what gets collected is documented, queryable, and deliberately limited. It\'s device management with the'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lights on. Which brings us to the question every employee secretly types into a search bar. What can my boss actually see on my laptop? Fleet\'s answer is unusually direct. Device data, installed software, OS versions,'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'security posture, hardware health, not keystrokes, not emails, not browsing history, not your webcam. And because the agent is open source, that claim is verifiable rather than a privacy policy promise. End users can literally audit'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the code that\'s watching their machine. In a category built on quiet surveillance anxiety, that transparency is the whole product. Coverage-wise, it\'s genuinely everything with a CPU. macOS, Windows, and Linux as equal'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first-class citizens. All major distros, which is rarer in MDM than it should be. Chromebooks, iOS and Android, corporate-owned or BYOD, cloud instances on AWS, GCP, and Azure, containers, even'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Linux-based IoT devices. One console, one agent story, one query language across the entire estate. If your company has it, Fleet can probably enroll it and query it 10 seconds later.'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The testimonial on their homepage says more than any feature list could. Wes Whetstone, client platform engineer at Stripe, "I just moved 10,000 Macs to Fleet." Sit with that. 10,000 company Macs at one of the most'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'security-obsessed companies in technology migrated onto an open-source MDM. That is the kind of reference check money cannot buy and marketing cannot fake. The website leans confidently into the numbers. 2,000,000 devices, 90 countries, a 21-minute average support'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'response time. And the documentation behind it is enterprise-grade. Deployment guides, the complete osquery table reference, CIS benchmark documentation, and a fully public company handbook that'

[05:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'explains in writing exactly why they\'re open-source and how the business works. Radical transparency as a sales strategy. GitHub tells the rest of the story. 6,711 stars, 980 forks, 467 contributors, and'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over 14 million Docker pulls. Nearly 27,000 commits, too. Though, to be fair, the website, docs, and that company handbook all live in the same monorepo.'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Version 4.90 shipped this month. The repository was pushed to this very day. And after nearly a decade of lineage, the pace is still compounding. The arc, on one slide, 2017.'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Kolide Fleet, the original open source osquery manager. 2020, after the original project went quiet, osquery co-creator Zach Wasserman and sales.js creator Mike McNeil founded Fleet Device Management to carry the'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'code and the community forward. 2026, version 4.90, 200,000 devices and some of the biggest names in tech on the customer page. This channel covers a lot of revival stories. This is what the fully grown'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version looks like. Now, the line I promised to draw. Fleet is open core. The core, real MDM, osquery visibility, scripting, patching, software deployment, GitOps, is genuinely free'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'under an MIT-style license, and it\'s what most of this video showed you. The EE directory holds Fleet Premium enterprise editions like certain integrations and advanced controls under a paid license, and Fleet\'s cloud'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hosting is a paid product. That\'s their business model, stated plainly in their own handbook. Self-hosting the free tier is real, but it\'s infrastructure. A MySQL database, Redis, and an agent rollout. An IT project, not a weekend'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'home lab toy. Though, plenty of home labbers run it anyway. And those 3,396 open issues? On a platform this size, that\'s not neglect, it\'s a public roadmap.'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everything in the open, including the backlog. So, that\'s Fleet, the MDM your employees can audit, your IT team can script, and your security lead can actually defend in a review. The repository, website, and docs are linked'

